---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/alternatives/postscript-alternative";
const title = "Postscript alternatives: preserve the sender, keywords and release queue";
const desc = "Compare replacing a live Postscript program with repairing it or moving only one channel. Start with the number and every place customers use it.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When is keeping Postscript sensible?",
    "answer": "Keep it when its acquisition, segmentation and reply workflow meet the requirement and the team can maintain them. Repair a specific data or scheduling defect before rebuilding the whole program. Current email access should be checked in the account rather than assumed absent."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can the phone number and keywords move together?",
    "answer": "Do not assume so. Obtain provider-specific instructions for the number, registration, keyword responses and timing. A subscriber CSV does not transfer a sender or reroute a printed keyword. Keep an owner for replies throughout the change."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can a separate email tool replace the SMS program?",
    "answer": "An email-only scope leaves the SMS sender, reply handling and acquisition routes unresolved. If evaluating Sequenzy for email, demonstrate its required data exchange and suppression first; no integration, price advantage or SMS replacement is established here."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<main class="container py-14 md:py-20">
<a class="link" href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a>
<p class="badge mt-6">Replacement planning · November 25, 2026</p>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
<p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Keep the working parts before choosing a substitute</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/postscript-sms-marketing">current Postscript listing</a> advertises Shopify-data segmentation, automations and two-way messaging, and describes SMS, RCS and phased email availability. These are positive reasons to retain an established account if they fit the work. An email gap may be an access or workflow question rather than a reason to replace SMS. Confirm what is enabled in your account.</p>
<p>This page addresses an existing program; the <a href="/apps/postscript">Postscript app review</a> provides an initial release trial. Here the decision is what must survive departure: the number customers recognize, keyword promises, permission history, replies and messages already waiting. Identify the actual defect and its owner. Fix an incorrect Shopify field or conflicting calendar before concluding that a new vendor will fix it.</p>
<h2>Make the number and keyword register the first deliverable</h2>
<p>Postscript's <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564366-what-is-my-phone-number-subscribers-can-text-to-opt-into-my-sms">number guide</a> describes a brand-specific toll-free number and incoming subscriber replies. Its <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564129-create-a-keyword">keyword guide</a> describes acquisition from banners, social, printed material and other placements. Moving contacts does not establish continuity for those entry routes.</p>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Existing asset</th><th>Record before departure</th><th>Acceptance question</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Sending number</td><td>Provider, type, registration owner and reply destination</td><td>Who confirms transfer or replacement, and when may it send?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keyword and response</td><td>Exact text, promise, eligibility and linked journey</td><td>What happens to a new and a returning subscriber?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Physical and digital placements</td><td>Packaging, QR destinations, social links, forms and printed stock</td><td>Can it be changed, redirected or serviced while old stock remains?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reply queue</td><td>Open requests, assignment and escalation path</td><td>Who sees an incoming help request during the handover?</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Ask both providers for written instructions for your exact sender and destinations. Do not treat a guide about migration into Postscript as proof of the reverse process. If a new number is required, plan how customers and staff will recognize it. Keep the existing route serviced or retire its placements deliberately; do not promise that old printed keywords will route to the new provider.</p>
<h2>Reconcile permission separately from segment membership</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13563865-understanding-subscriber-opt-in-sources">opt-in-source guide</a> distinguishes keyword, form, manual upload and checkout sources; its analytics count excludes people who joined and opted out within the selected period. The <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13563863-view-subscribers-included-in-a-segment">segment guide</a> describes exporting segment members. Neither makes a campaign audience an exhaustive permission ledger.</p>
<p>Request the available evidence, exclusions and historical statuses with field definitions. Reconcile active, withdrawn, unknown and duplicate records separately using stable identifiers and channel-specific permissions. Keep disclosure versions, capture source and timestamps where available; document missing evidence instead of reconstructing it. A phone number, email subscription or past order alone is not authority to import someone as SMS subscribed.</p>
<p>In a proposed test fixture, include an active keyword signup, a withdrawn contact, an email-only buyer, a changed phone number and two records sharing an identifier. Show the import disposition and eligible audience for each. Then withdraw permission after the export checkpoint and show that the destination receives the change before any send. Review <a href="/guides/consent">consent planning</a> for the evidence record.</p>
<h2>Shortlist by the job that is moving</h2>
<p>Use an unranked demonstration brief. Ask <a href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> and <a href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> to demonstrate an email/SMS audience with the same purchase exit and ownership rules. Ask <a href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a> to demonstrate the required acquisition and reply service and state who performs the work. For broader message requirements, ask <a href="/apps/brevo">Brevo</a> to show the exact Shopify events and destinations. These are procurement tasks, not claims of feature parity or superior outcomes.</p>
<p>If moving only email, leave the accepted SMS operation in scope with a named owner. Evaluate <a href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> or another email candidate against education, welcome and winback requirements without assuming a native connection. Require a field map, withdrawal path and collision test. A partial replacement should not accidentally change the sender or reactivate a welcome journey.</p>
<h2>Carry the remaining release work, not every historical trigger</h2>
<p>For an illustrative limited release, preserve size or style interest, waitlist priority and VIP access without automatically adding a discount. Snapshot who has already received access, who purchased and who is still waiting. A pending release message is a remaining obligation, not a reason to replay its entire flow. Link to the exact variant and verify availability immediately before the owned send; a scheduled scarcity claim can become false.</p>
<p>Exercise a purchase during a wait, a sold-out variant, a withdrawn subscriber and a delayed order event. Confirm cancellation and inspect the next eligible action. Coordinate email education and SMS access through one calendar owner. Subscription renewals, open support requests and gift versus self-purchase messages need explicit treatment rather than a universal monthly cap. Use <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a>, <a href="/use-cases/back-in-stock">waitlist planning</a> and <a href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline</a>.</p>
<h2>Compare complete costs and contribution</h2>
<p>Obtain current terms from <a href="https://postscript.io/pricing">Postscript pricing</a> and each candidate. Separate subscription, minimum-spend treatment, SMS/MMS units, carrier charges, destinations, number work, reply staffing and migration labor. Preview actual copy, links and characters to determine billable units; one recipient does not necessarily equal one billed message. Model the peak release month and projected list growth.</p>
<p>Illustrative order math: $100 merchandise value less $10 incentive gives $90 collected; less $45 goods cost leaves $45 before messaging, fulfillment, returns and support. Do not subtract the same discount again from already discounted net sales. Compare a suitable eligible holdout over the same window where feasible, noting that sold-out inventory affects later purchase opportunity. Attributed revenue alone does not establish incremental benefit. Keep the <a href="/guides/pricing">cost worksheet</a> alongside the quote.</p>
<h2>Approve one sender per job and a recoverable checkpoint</h2>
<ol><li>Save the asset register, permission reconciliation and remaining-step queue with an explicit checkpoint.</li><li>Agree which system owns acquisition, replies, each flow and each scheduled broadcast. Pause outgoing jobs and verify what happened to queued messages.</li><li>With test contacts, exercise new signup, purchase exit, withdrawal, reply handling and recipient-time sending restrictions. Confirm current market rules with the provider; no single time window covers every destination.</li><li>At activation, replay only verified changes since the checkpoint, including purchases and withdrawals. Keep duplicate jobs disabled.</li><li>If reverting, reconcile completed sends and new changes before resuming the old system. Reverting a configuration is not permission to resend completed access messages.</li></ol>
<p>Keep an exception log and a named pause owner. These are proposed acceptance checks, not observed migration results or a promise of uninterrupted transfer. If the number, evidence or remaining queue cannot be reconciled, retain the accepted operation while resolving that specific issue. Continue with <a href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">Postscript and Attentive decision work</a> or <a href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> according to the unresolved job.</p>

</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</main>
</BaseLayout>
